# Why Do Promises Run Before Timers?

Promise callbacks are placed into the **microtask queue**.

Timer callbacks are placed into the **task queue**.

After the current synchronous code finishes, the JavaScript environment processes pending microtasks before moving on to the next task.

That's why:

```javascript
Promise.resolve().then(...)
```

usually runs before:

```javascript
setTimeout(..., 0)
```

when both are scheduled from the same synchronous execution.

---

# Event Loop and User Clicks

The Event Loop isn't only about timers.

Consider:

```javascript
button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    console.log("Button clicked");
});
```

When the user clicks the button:

```text
User clicks
    ↓
Browser detects event
    ↓
Callback becomes eligible
    ↓
Event Loop
    ↓
Call Stack
    ↓
Callback executes
```

This is how JavaScript can respond to user interaction while handling other browser tasks.

---

# Why JavaScript Doesn't Freeze

Imagine:

```javascript
fetch("/users")
```

The browser can handle the network operation while JavaScript continues with other work.

Later, when the response is ready, the associated Promise continuation can be scheduled.

Conceptually:

```text
JavaScript
    |
    | fetch()
    ↓
Browser handles network
    |
    | JavaScript continues
    ↓
Other code executes
    |
    | Response arrives
    ↓
Promise callback becomes ready
    |
    ↓
Event Loop
    |
    ↓
JavaScript executes callback
```

---

# The Event Loop Does NOT Make JavaScript Multithreaded

This is a common misunderstanding.

The Event Loop does not mean:

> "JavaScript executes everything at the same time."

Instead, it coordinates when callbacks can be executed on the JavaScript thread.

The browser itself has many internal mechanisms and may use multiple threads, but JavaScript execution on the main thread follows its own execution model.

---

# Blocking the Call Stack

Consider:

```javascript
function slowTask() {

    for (let i = 0; i < 10000000000; i++) {
        // heavy work
    }
}

slowTask();
```

This keeps the JavaScript thread busy.

While this code is executing, other JavaScript callbacks cannot simply interrupt it.

The page may become unresponsive.

---

# Why Heavy JavaScript Can Cause Lag

Imagine:

```text
Heavy JavaScript
       ↓
Call Stack stays busy
       ↓
Callbacks wait
       ↓
User interactions may feel delayed
       ↓
Page becomes unresponsive
```

This is why expensive JavaScript work should be handled carefully.

---

# Long Tasks

A **long task** is JavaScript work that occupies the main thread for a noticeable amount of time.

For example:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000000; i++) {
    // expensive calculation
}
```

During this work, the browser may have difficulty responding smoothly to:

* Clicks
* Typing
* Animations
* Scrolling
* Other JavaScript events

---

# Breaking Up Heavy Work

Instead of doing a huge amount of work in one synchronous block, work can sometimes be divided into smaller pieces.

For example:

```javascript
function processChunk(start, end) {

    for (let i = start; i < end; i++) {
        // work
    }

    if (end < 1000) {
        setTimeout(() => {
            processChunk(end, end + 100);
        }, 0);
    }
}
```

This gives the browser opportunities to process other work between chunks.

For truly CPU-heavy work, technologies such as **Web Workers** can also be considered.

