# Simple Architecture

A simplified model:

```text
                 JavaScript
                     |
                     ↓
                Call Stack
                     |
        ┌────────────┴────────────┐
        ↓                         ↓
    Web APIs                 Synchronous Code
        |
        ↓
  ┌───────────────┐
  │               │
Task Queue    Microtask Queue
  │               │
  └───────┬───────┘
          ↓
      Event Loop
          ↓
     Call Stack
```

---

# What You Should Remember

### Call Stack

Executes JavaScript code.

### Web APIs

Browser-provided functionality such as timers, DOM events, and networking.

### Task Queue

Holds callbacks waiting to be executed as tasks.

### Microtask Queue

Holds Promise callbacks and other microtasks.

### Event Loop

Coordinates when queued work can move to the Call Stack.

---

# Key Takeaways

* JavaScript execution on the main thread is single-threaded.
* The Call Stack executes synchronous JavaScript.
* Browser APIs handle many asynchronous operations.
* Asynchronous callbacks don't automatically execute immediately.
* They are scheduled to run when the JavaScript thread is available.
* Promise callbacks use the microtask queue.
* Timers and many browser events use task queues.
* Microtasks are processed before moving to the next task.
* `setTimeout(..., 0)` does not mean immediate execution.
* Heavy synchronous JavaScript can block the main thread.
* Blocking the main thread can make a webpage feel slow or unresponsive.
* The Event Loop coordinates asynchronous work; it doesn't make JavaScript itself magically multithreaded.

---

## Quick Mental Model

```text
Synchronous JavaScript
        ↓
    Call Stack
        ↓
   Code finishes
        ↓
  Microtask Queue
        ↓
    Event Loop
        ↓
     Task Queue
        ↓
    Call Stack
```

### In One Sentence

> **The JavaScript Event Loop coordinates the Call Stack, asynchronous browser operations, and task queues so JavaScript can handle asynchronous work without blocking the entire webpage.**
